So here,

36
00:01:35,310 --> 00:01:40,450
before we continue further development,

37
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:42,510
we need to take the memory

38
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:44,510
and manage it further.

39
00:01:44,510 --> 00:01:45,570
Why is that?

40
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:47,470
It's because from what we saw earlier,

41
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:48,830
What we've been learning is that

42
00:01:48,830 --> 00:01:51,530
when it comes to memory management,

43
00:01:51,530 --> 00:01:54,230
up to this point,

44
00:01:54,230 --> 00:01:55,710
we've actually already encountered

45
00:01:55,710 --> 00:01:57,530
two kinds of memory management approaches,

46
00:01:57,530 --> 00:01:57,810
right?

47
00:01:57,810 --> 00:02:00,730
The first one is called automatic memory management.

48
00:02:00,730 --> 00:02:02,210
Now, this way of managing memory,

49
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:04,450
we actually implement through the stack,

50
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:06,690
through this mechanism.

51
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:08,990
This actually corresponds in our C language,

52
00:02:08,990 --> 00:02:10,870
to what is essentially used,

53
00:02:10,870 --> 00:02:13,190
namely the automatic variables defined inside functions.

54
00:02:13,190 --> 00:02:14,690
Now, we know that these automatic variables,

55
00:02:14,690 --> 00:02:15,290
this kind of memory,

56
00:02:15,290 --> 00:02:18,190
when you enter a function,

57
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:21,250
it gets allocated inside the function stack,

58
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:22,350
and when the function exits,

59
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:24,210
it is actually released,

60
00:02:24,210 --> 00:02:24,430
right?

61
00:02:24,430 --> 00:02:25,810
Once it goes out of scope, it's released.

62
00:02:25,810 --> 00:02:27,190
You can think of it as a kind of automatic management.

63
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:28,450
Now, there's another kind.

64
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:30,290
What we're studying is static memory.

65
00:02:30,290 --> 00:02:33,030
In our code, this corresponds to,

66
00:02:33,030 --> 00:02:34,850
basically, defining some global variables,

67
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:35,930
or static variables.

68
00:02:35,930 --> 00:02:37,230
Now, for these variables,

69
00:02:37,230 --> 00:02:40,010
we're actually using them in a static way.

70
00:02:40,010 --> 00:02:41,630
We know where this static memory is placed.

71
00:02:41,630 --> 00:02:44,310
It's actually placed after we compile,

72
00:02:44,310 --> 00:02:47,090
in some special data segments.

73
00:02:47,090 --> 00:02:47,710
Right?

74
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:48,630
In the data sections.

75
00:02:48,630 --> 00:02:49,570
Then they get loaded into memory,

76
00:02:49,570 --> 00:02:50,810
placed in these data segments.

77
00:02:50,810 --> 00:02:52,230
Okay.

78
00:02:52,230 --> 00:02:54,170
So, for these things,

79
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:55,650
what we can see is that,

80
00:02:55,650 --> 00:02:58,350
beyond this way of using memory,

81
00:02:58,350 --> 00:03:00,590
Actually, there's another kind of

82
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:02,210
demand for memory

83
00:03:02,210 --> 00:03:03,310
which is that we might need to

84
00:03:03,310 --> 00:03:04,650
at runtime

85
00:03:04,650 --> 00:03:07,070
dynamically allocate some memory.

86
00:03:07,070 --> 00:03:09,770
Because in real-world programming,

87
00:03:09,770 --> 00:03:12,230
we know there are some data structures

88
00:03:12,230 --> 00:03:13,570
whose memory size

89
00:03:13,570 --> 00:03:16,190
actually, at the point when the program starts running,

90
00:03:16,190 --> 00:03:18,270
cannot simply be placed

91
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:20,030
entirely into static memory,

92
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:21,930
or stored on the stack.

93
00:03:21,930 --> 00:03:24,490
So in C, for example,

94
00:03:24,490 --> 00:03:25,410
what we have inside is, say,

95
00:03:25,410 --> 00:03:26,210
we use malloc,

96
00:03:26,210 --> 00:03:27,650
or free, that kind of

97
00:03:27,650 --> 00:03:28,590
dynamic approach.

98
00:03:28,590 --> 00:03:30,150
Now, this dynamic approach

99
00:03:30,150 --> 00:03:32,030
brings in the concept of a heap.

100
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:33,730
And this heap,

101
00:03:33,730 --> 00:03:35,250
under the hood, exactly where

102
00:03:35,250 --> 00:03:37,070
So, for example, when we

103
00:03:37,070 --> 00:03:38,270
normally call malloc

104
00:03:38,270 --> 00:03:38,870
and free,

105
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:40,310
behind that, exactly where

106
00:03:40,310 --> 00:03:42,750
is that heap located in memory?

107
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:44,790
Well, in fact, in our

108
00:03:44,790 --> 00:03:46,730
at the very lowest level,

109
00:03:46,730 --> 00:03:48,170
it's actually maintained for you

110
00:03:48,170 --> 00:03:49,770
by our operating system.

111
00:03:49,770 --> 00:03:51,730
So how do we

112
00:03:51,730 --> 00:03:53,710
build that heap?

113
00:03:53,710 --> 00:03:54,470
Today,

114
00:03:54,470 --> 00:03:55,950
we'll mainly focus on that topic.

115
00:03:55,950 --> 00:04:00,990
Alright, let's first look at this table.

116
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:02,490
We've seen this table several times before,

117
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:03,330
the memory map.

118
00:04:03,330 --> 00:04:05,050
From what we learned earlier,

119
00:04:05,050 --> 00:04:07,150
we know that on this table,

120
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:08,550
the Bootloader ROM region

121
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:09,650
We actually can't touch it.

122
00:04:09,650 --> 00:04:11,050
Because you can think of it as

123
00:04:11,050 --> 00:04:12,930
a block of read-only memory that's baked in there.

124
00:04:12,930 --> 00:04:16,050
What we actually work with more is the DRAM.

125
00:04:16,050 --> 00:04:16,950
That is, this block up here.

126
00:04:16,950 --> 00:04:18,030
This block up here.

127
00:04:18,030 --> 00:04:19,270
Now for this block up here,

128
00:04:19,270 --> 00:04:20,890
we know that after going through the mapping,

129
00:04:20,890 --> 00:04:23,230
it gets mapped to our physical memory,

130
00:04:23,230 --> 00:04:29,530
from 0x8000 up to 0x88000000,

131
00:04:29,530 --> 00:04:31,790
over this range of addresses.

132
00:04:31,790 --> 00:04:33,630
Now for the memory in this address range,

133
00:04:33,630 --> 00:04:36,170
actually, how it's used internally,

134
00:04:36,170 --> 00:04:38,370
we haven't done any deeper management of it.

135
00:04:38,370 --> 00:04:38,990
Think about it, right?

136
00:04:38,990 --> 00:04:43,230
Because as you know, let's look at this diagram down here.

137
00:04:43,230 --> 00:04:48,290
Actually, for this whole area on this side,

138
00:04:48,290 --> 00:04:52,210
like on this Virtual board we're currently using,

139
00:04:52,210 --> 00:04:54,750
for this memory in this 128-megabyte range,

140
00:04:54,750 --> 00:04:57,270
we haven't actually done any further or deeper management.

141
00:04:57,270 --> 00:04:58,450
Why do I say that?

142
00:04:58,450 --> 00:04:59,510
Well, as you know,

143
00:04:59,510 --> 00:05:02,610
after we compile a program,

144
00:05:02,610 --> 00:05:04,330
and those of you who did the earlier ones,

145
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:08,790
you know that what we compile here is actually an OS.ELF,

146
00:05:08,790 --> 00:05:11,030
an executable file like that.

147
00:05:11,030 --> 00:05:12,750
Now, this file, as we know,

148
00:05:12,750 --> 00:05:14,070
based on what we covered earlier,

