00:05:14,070 --> 00:05:16,030
is actually in ELF format,

150
00:05:16,030 --> 00:05:16,330
right?

151
00:05:16,330 --> 00:05:18,630
And in this ELF format,

152
00:05:18,630 --> 00:05:21,250
there are corresponding sections.

153
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:23,190
Then during loading,

154
00:05:23,190 --> 00:05:24,670
it is loaded segment by segment.

155
00:05:24,670 --> 00:05:25,470
When this data,

156
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:27,050
is loaded into memory,

157
00:05:27,050 --> 00:05:29,630
it will actually only take up

158
00:05:29,630 --> 00:05:31,230
a portion of our 128 megabytes,

159
00:05:31,230 --> 00:05:32,410
part of the space,

160
00:05:32,410 --> 00:05:35,330
and the rest

161
00:05:35,330 --> 00:05:37,170
There is actually a large chunk of space

162
00:05:37,170 --> 00:05:37,910
This chunk of space

163
00:05:37,910 --> 00:05:38,970
we actually have not

164
00:05:38,970 --> 00:05:40,610
made use of it

165
00:05:40,610 --> 00:05:42,430
So later on,

166
00:05:42,430 --> 00:05:44,030
for the dynamic allocation we want to do,

167
00:05:44,030 --> 00:05:46,150
the idea is basically to find a way

168
00:05:46,150 --> 00:05:48,090
to use this memory at the back,

169
00:05:48,090 --> 00:05:50,330
which is what we call the heap,

170
00:05:50,330 --> 00:05:51,970
this memory heap,

171
00:05:51,970 --> 00:05:53,890
and put it to use.

172
00:05:53,890 --> 00:05:55,690
And by using a dynamic approach,

173
00:05:55,690 --> 00:05:57,170
we can, in our programs,

174
00:05:57,170 --> 00:05:58,890
allocate when we need it,

175
00:05:58,890 --> 00:06:00,530
and return it when we no longer need it.

176
00:06:00,530 --> 00:06:02,730
For the sections below, as we know,

177
00:06:02,730 --> 00:06:03,870
once they are loaded into memory,

178
00:06:03,870 --> 00:06:06,070
their size cannot be changed.

179
00:06:06,070 --> 00:06:11,210
So if we want to make use of this part,

180
00:06:11,210 --> 00:06:15,270
then the next question arises

181
00:06:15,270 --> 00:06:17,130
So if we want to make use of this region,

182
00:06:17,130 --> 00:06:18,630
what do we need to do first?

183
00:06:18,630 --> 00:06:18,990
As you all know,

184
00:06:18,990 --> 00:06:20,130
first you have to know

185
00:06:20,130 --> 00:06:21,150
where this region

186
00:06:21,150 --> 00:06:23,090
actually starts,

187
00:06:23,090 --> 00:06:24,570
and how much space is actually left.

188
00:06:24,570 --> 00:06:26,550
These are all things we need to know,

189
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:28,150
because only after knowing this information

190
00:06:28,150 --> 00:06:30,250
can we take this chunk of memory

191
00:06:30,250 --> 00:06:32,850
and manage it in a finer-grained way.

192
00:06:32,850 --> 00:06:34,810
Now, some of you might say,

193
00:06:34,810 --> 00:06:36,870
can't we just calculate it ourselves?

194
00:06:36,870 --> 00:06:39,390
Well, of course you can.

195
00:06:39,390 --> 00:06:41,170
Because in our...

196
00:06:41,170 --> 00:06:42,270
But of course, that's really cumbersome,

197
00:06:42,270 --> 00:06:43,370
because basically you'd have to know

198
00:06:43,370 --> 00:06:44,250
the sizes of all the variables

199
00:06:44,250 --> 00:06:45,630
that were allocated in there,

200
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:47,810
and then compute it yourself based on that.

201
00:06:47,810 --> 00:06:50,110
But in fact, there's really no need to do that.

202
00:06:50,110 --> 00:06:52,550
Because as everyone knows, once you have a compiler and a linker,

203
00:06:52,550 --> 00:06:54,270
during the compilation and linking process,

204
00:06:54,270 --> 00:06:57,490
the compiler and linker can actually figure out these sizes for you automatically.

205
00:06:57,490 --> 00:07:00,370
We don't need to calculate them by hand.

206
00:07:00,370 --> 00:07:02,450
So what we'll do next is look at

207
00:07:02,450 --> 00:07:05,990
how to get the size of this region and its starting address.

208
00:07:05,990 --> 00:07:08,630
At the same time, we actually make full use of

209
00:07:08,630 --> 00:07:11,610
some features of the compiler and linker to help us do this.

210
00:07:11,610 --> 00:07:16,150
So let's now

211
00:07:16,150 --> 00:07:18,590
briefly review

212
00:07:18,590 --> 00:07:20,150
the compilation and linking process,

213
00:07:20,150 --> 00:07:21,730
as well as some related concepts.

